## Mandatory Parallelism Policy

At any safe boundary, if two or more independent graph-unblocked tasks/streams exist, launch parallel subagents/workstreams.

Safe boundaries:
- goal start;
- after board/stream ingestion;
- after a ticket handoff;
- after a review approval or block;
- after resolving a blocker;
- before claiming next work;
- on every 1-minute Kanban poll;
- when restarting after stall;
- when the user, reviewer, or Kanban sends a heartbeat.

Decision rule:

```text
if blocked_codex_tickets_in_project_scope >= 1:
    transition the highest-priority/current blocked card only to In Progress
    resume its original worker or dispatch one continuity replacement
elif current_codex_card_is_triage_due_to_block_loop_detected:
    recover only that same card to In Progress
    resume its original worker or dispatch one continuity replacement
elif ready_or_running_codex_tickets_in_project_scope == 0:
    report no Kanban-backed `[Codex]` work inside project scope and stop
elif safe_parallel_streams >= 2:
    dispatch one PowerShell lane orchestrator per safe stream
else:
    work the single stream or route the blocker
```

Acceptable reasons not to parallelize:
- file/hunk overlap;
- shared public contract unresolved;
- schema/migration ordering;
- architecture boundary unresolved;
- dirty worktree prevents safe isolation;
- resource constraints for heavy tests/builds;
- reviewer/user explicitly directed serial execution;
- only one graph-unblocked stream exists.

Unacceptable reasons:
- "simpler";
- "not necessary";
- "I can do it myself";
- "I forgot";
- "coordination overhead";
- "parallel agents might be overkill" without a concrete safety reason.

If parallelism is available but not dispatched, record:

```text
PARALLELISM EXCEPTION:
- streams considered:
- graph-unblocked tasks:
- reason parallelism is unsafe:
- conflicts:
- selected single stream:
- what would make parallelism safe:
```

Post this as a task comment or heartbeat when the workflow has a Kanban flight recorder and it is appropriate.

## Workstream Packet

Every subagent/workstream must receive a strict packet:

```text
WORKSTREAM PACKET

Board:
<board>

Workspace:
<workspace path>

Task:
<task_id> - <title containing [Codex], or body explicitly marked [Codex]>

Stream:
<stream name>

Goal:
<one concrete goal>

Project scope:
- <workspace/project root and any narrower allowed directories>

Allowed files/directories:
- <paths>

Forbidden files/directories:
- <paths>

Dependencies:
- Parents:
- Children:
- Stream terminal:
- Integration gates waiting on this:

Can run in parallel with:
- <streams/tasks>

Must serialize with:
- <streams/tasks and why>

Guardrails:
- Work only on this `[Codex]` ticket.
- Touch only files inside Project scope and Allowed files/directories.
- Do not edit sibling apps, parent repos, home-directory skill/config files, generated dependency folders, or external worktrees unless they are explicitly inside this ticket's Project scope.
- Finish exactly this task/slice.
- Do not bundle adjacent tickets.
- Do not mark Done; Hermes alone owns Done.
- Do not push unless explicitly authorized.
- Do not delete runtime/database data.
- Do not fabricate evidence, tests, outputs, product data, or runtime state.
- Test fixtures/mocks are allowed only when clearly test-only.
- If architecture/API/schema/security boundaries are unclear, do not cross them; report the exact boundary and continue safe same-card work inside the existing scope.

Verification:
- <narrowest relevant command>

Commit policy:
- Commit only intended files if the workflow requires commits.
- Do not stage unrelated files.
- Report dirty/untracked files separately.

Report format:
- summary:
- files changed:
- tests/checks run with exact output:
- commit SHA if any:
- blockers:
- risks:
- forbidden scope avoided:
- project scope respected:
- next unblocked task(s):
```

Coordinator responsibilities:
- choose launch batch;
- spawn one separate PowerShell lane orchestrator per safe independent lane;
- assign file ownership;
- prevent collisions;
- collect outputs;
- run or sequence heavy tests;
- integrate one stream at a time;
- post or prepare separate review handoffs;
- update Kanban comments/heartbeats as required.

For detailed subagent dispatch rules and a narrower DebateAI/Hermes-oriented template, see `references/subagents.md`.

## File Ownership and Conflict Policy

Before dispatching parallel workstreams, assign file ownership.

Rules:
- Two write workstreams must not edit the same file by default.
- If overlap is likely, serialize or make one workstream read-only.
- Shared config/schema/API files create a contract boundary; route or serialize.
- Docs-only streams can run beside code streams if files do not overlap.
- Read-only review, audit, and test-planning streams can run in parallel freely.
- Heavy builds/tests should be sequenced unless cheap and isolated.
- Re-check git status before and after integrating each workstream.
- Never stage unrelated/generated files.

Conflict template:

```text
FILE CONFLICT ROUTING:
- conflicting streams:
- conflicting files:
- chosen owner:
- deferred/serialized stream:
- reason:
- next safe action:
```

## Review and Done Ownership

Hermes exclusively owns Done:
- Codex never marks Done and never calls the Kanban completion command.
- Codex submits a review handoff only.
- Hermes approves and marks Done, or blocks/returns the same card.
- A Hermes block immediately sends only that same card back to In Progress for Codex rework.
- Codex may continue independent safe streams while waiting only when the board and resource law authorize them.

General handoff:

```text
READY FOR REVIEW:
- task:
- stream:
- summary:
- files changed:
- commit SHA:
- tests/checks:
  - command:
  - exact output:
- subagents/workstreams used:
- risks/blockers:
- fake evidence/data check:
- dirty/untracked files:
- next believed unblocked task(s):
```

If the project requires exact marker text, use that exact marker. For Hermes-style review, use:

```text
READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:
```

Parallel review rules:
- Separate tasks get separate handoffs.
- Do not collapse unrelated streams into one mega-handoff.
- Reviewers can review multiple independent tickets if handoffs are clean.
- Children/gates should not start until parents are approved unless the graph/workflow explicitly allows it.

Use `references/packet-formats.md` when exact READY/BLOCKED/STALLED/HEARTBEAT packet formats are required.

## Autonomous Handoff and Wait Loop

After launch, keep the recurring Kanban watch loop alive. After completing a wave, do not exit, do not wait for the user manually, and do not treat chat as the source of truth. Handoff means continue the Kanban polling/await loop.

When a wave is finished:

1. For each completed ticket, post a Kanban comment beginning exactly:

```text
READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:
```

2. Include for each ticket:
   - ticket ID/title;
   - files changed;
   - exact tests/checks run;
   - commit SHA;
   - risks/blockers;
   - subagents/workstreams used;
   - next believed unblocked ticket.
3. Do not mark tickets Done. Hermes owns Done; Codex owns returning Blocked cards to In Progress for rework.
4. After posting `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:`, enter the polling loop.

Polling loop:
- Poll once immediately after heartbeat-protocol launch, then every 1 minute while the session remains active unless current V/Hermes authority specifies another cadence.
- After each handoff, blocker, Hermes approval, Hermes return, or dependency-stream transition, poll immediately, then continue the 1-minute cadence.
- Inspect all in-scope `[Codex]` Ready/Running/Review/For-review/Blocked tickets and recent comments, including the same tickets handed off.
- Look specifically for Hermes review results:
  - ticket status changed to done;
  - `HERMES REVIEW BLOCKER` comment;
  - `HERMES ROUTING HOLD` comment;
  - new Ready child/promoted task;
  - explicit Hermes routing comment.

If Hermes approved tickets and new safe `[Codex]` Ready work exists inside project scope:
- inspect dependencies/comments for the next tickets;
- choose the next small safe wave only from Ready/Running tickets explicitly marked `[Codex]` and inside the declared project scope;
- avoid overlapping file scopes/hunks;
- avoid simultaneous heavy tests/builds;
- dispatch subagents/workstreams where practical;
- continue implementation.

If Hermes blocked a ticket:
- read the `HERMES REVIEW BLOCKER` comment;
- classify it as local/dependency/process/safety;
- immediately transition only the same card from Blocked to In Progress;
- route the blocker back to the same lane orchestrator and the same coding subagent/session that implemented the ticket whenever technically possible;
- if the original session is unavailable, launch exactly one replacement continuity with the full card, diff, tests, and blocker history; record the continuity break;
- fix only the requested slice;
- rerun focused verification;
- commit;
- post `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:` again;
- do not mark Done.

Hermes return-to-same-subagent rule:
- Maintain a ticket-to-lane-orchestrator and ticket-to-coding-subagent routing table for all active `[Codex]` tickets.
- When Hermes returns a ticket with requested fixes, do not create a fresh unrelated implementer by default.
- Resume the original coding subagent/session for that ticket if the environment supports it.
- If the original coding subagent cannot be resumed, the lane orchestrator must assign the replacement as a continuity repair, provide the original packet, prior diff, prior review comment, and exact blocker, and state in the next handoff that the original session could not be resumed.
- After the coding subagent fixes the ticket, the lane orchestrator must review the fix before posting `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:` again.
- The main coordinator only routes the returned ticket to the responsible lane orchestrator and checks that the Hermes handoff was reposted; it does not bypass the lane review step.

If Hermes posted a routing hold or process blocker:
- post one evidence-backed `CODEX BLOCKED` or short blocker comment on the affected ticket;
- transition only the same card from Blocked to In Progress and keep its worker active;
- investigate and fix every part that is already authorized inside the current card scope instead of waiting idle;
- if one exact operation remains forbidden or approval-gated, avoid only that operation, state the smallest operation gate, and continue safe read-only or reversible work on the same card;
- do not route a successor or launch a duplicate writer.

If no Hermes response is visible yet:
- do not start unrelated new work;
- do not spam long updates;
- post at most one short heartbeat every 10-15 minutes:

```text
CODEX HEARTBEAT:
- current ticket/wave:
- state: waiting_for_hermes_review
- last command/check:
- files changed: none since handoff
- subagents active/completed:
- needs Hermes: yes
```

Continue this loop until:
- the overall goal is done;
- Hermes/V explicitly pauses Codex;
- a safety/process blocker requires a human decision;
- the worktree becomes dirty/unclear in a way Codex cannot classify safely.

Important:
- "Await Hermes input" means poll Kanban/Heartbeat comments every 1 minute, not stop the session.
- V should not need to press Resume after every wave.
- V should not need to relay routine DebateAI routing details while Hermes is the cockpit broker.
- Kanban is source of truth.
- If Kanban has no Ready, Running, or Blocked `[Codex]` implementation tickets inside the declared project scope, Codex must tell V/user that no Kanban-backed Codex work is available and stop. Do not substitute pasted plans, PRDs, chat memory, or inferred lanes for missing Kanban tickets.
- Hermes owns review and Done. Codex owns same-card reactivation and rework after Blocked.
- Codex owns implementation and clean `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:` handoffs.

## Heartbeat Policy

Use short heartbeats at safe boundaries. Heartbeats are not long plans. If ready for review, use the review handoff marker. If blocked, use a blocker report.

```text
COORDINATION HEARTBEAT:
- current task(s):
- state: idle | mapping | parallel_working | working | ready_for_review | blocked | stalled
- active streams:
  - <stream/task>: <state>
- last command/check:
- files changed or none:
- needs reviewer/router: yes/no
- parallelism:
  - streams considered:
  - active parallel streams:
  - exceptions:
```

If the Kanban tool only allows heartbeats on running tasks, use the closest appropriate comment mechanism instead.

## Blocker Policy

Classify blockers:
- Local blocker: belongs to the current task, fixable by a small workstream, and should not stop unrelated independent streams.
- Dependency blocker: needs a parent/prerequisite stream; current task pauses while independent streams may continue.
- Process blocker: confusing Kanban state, contradictory instructions, dirty worktree, duplicate tasks, wrong Ready/Running state, or unclear Done ownership.
- Safety blocker: destructive action risk, secret leak risk, fake runtime data risk, broad unrelated refactor, unauthorized push, or unauthorized production/runtime data change.

Blocker template:

```text
BLOCKED:
- blocker type: local | dependency | process | safety
- task:
- stream:
- exact blocker:
- evidence:
- smallest safe fix/proposal:
- can dispatch subagent/workstream: yes/no
- can unrelated streams continue: yes/no
- needs reviewer/router/user: yes/no
```

Rules:
- On any blocker, post a Kanban comment on the affected ticket beginning exactly `CODEX BLOCKED:`. Hermes must be able to read the blocker without relying on chat history.
- Then move only the same `[Codex]` card from Blocked to In Progress and resume its worker. Comment on a downstream/parent/gate ticket too when the dependency belongs there, but keep the active card working rather than parking it.
- Include exact blocker evidence, the smallest safe proposal, whether unrelated streams can continue, and whether Hermes/V routing is needed.
- If a valid active `[Codex]` ticket exists, do not block the whole goal because of worktree path confusion, branch uncertainty, prior completed ticket state, or local implementation friction. Continue the active ticket, fix the smallest local slice, or post `CODEX HEARTBEAT` with `needs Hermes: yes`.
- If the Kanban comment command fails, retry once. If it still fails, report the failed command/error in chat or the nearest available comment surface, and do not pretend Hermes was notified.
- Fix local blockers with small workstreams when safe.
- Route dependency, process, and safety decisions while continuing every safe, authorized part of the same card. A decision gate blocks only the gated operation, not the whole work loop.
- Never use Blocked status alone as a terminal condition for the card, thread goal, or worker.
- Do not freeze all parallel work for one local blocker if unrelated streams are safe.
- Freeze shared-contract work if a blocker affects public API, schema, security, or architecture.

## Architecture and Contract Boundaries

Do not cross an unapproved boundary. Route the decision while continuing safe read-only analysis, tests, or in-scope alternatives when encountering:
- new subsystem or module boundary;
- public API contract changes;
- DB schema or migration changes;
- job/background worker orchestration;
- provider/adapter interface changes;
- auth/security behavior;
- data deletion or migration risk;
- cross-cutting frontend/backend state architecture;
- production/runtime data assumptions;
- ambiguous `decide`, `strategy`, `architecture`, or `phase` ticket wording.

Boundary template:

```text
ARCHITECTURE ROUTING NEEDED:
- task:
- stream:
- boundary:
- why current task may/may not authorize it:
- options:
- recommended smallest safe next task:
- can unrelated streams continue:
- needs reviewer/user:
```

## Dirty Worktree and Commit Hygiene

At start:

```powershell
git status --short --branch
```

Before commit:

```powershell
git status --short
git diff --check
```

After commit:

```powershell
git status --short --branch
git log -1 --oneline
```

Rules:
- Preserve pre-existing dirty state.
- Do not stage unrelated files.
- Do not commit generated junk unless explicitly required.
- If workflow requires commits, every review handoff should include commit SHA.
- If a commit is required and missing, the task is not review-ready.
- If worktree is dirty at start, record it before editing.

Dirty notice:

```text
DIRTY WORKTREE NOTICE:
- initial status:
- pre-existing dirty/untracked files:
- files this stream may touch:
- files this stream must not stage:
- safe to proceed: yes/no
```

## Verification Policy

- Run the narrowest relevant checks per task.
- Do not fabricate command output.
- If verification cannot run, report the exact blocker.
- Avoid simultaneous heavy tests/builds.
- Use lane-local tests for implementation streams.
- Use broader checks only at integration/milestone gates.
- Include exact command and real output in handoff.

Verification report:

```text
VERIFICATION:
- command:
- exit code:
- output summary:
- full relevant output:
- artifacts/generated files:
```

## Multi-Stream Completion Flow

When parallel workstreams finish:
1. Coordinator collects reports.
2. Coordinator checks git status.
3. Coordinator integrates one stream at a time.
4. Coordinator runs narrow verification for each stream.
5. Coordinator commits per task if required.
6. Coordinator posts/prepares separate review handoffs per task.
7. Coordinator re-reads Kanban after each approval, block, or handoff.
8. Coordinator launches newly unblocked independent `[Codex]` streams inside declared project scope if safe.
9. Coordinator does not start integration gates until all required stream terminals are approved/done.

If several streams finish at once, prefer separate commits, separate handoffs, separate evidence, and explicit next-task mapping.

## Checklists

Start checklist:
- [ ] Board/workspace identified.
- [ ] Declared project/workspace scope identified.
- [ ] Recurring Kanban watch loop started: first poll immediately, then every 1 minute while active unless current V/Hermes authority specifies another cadence.
- [ ] Kanban Ready/Running/Review/Blocked inspected.
- [ ] Tickets filtered to explicit `[Codex]` ownership before implementation selection.
- [ ] Every Blocked `[Codex]` card was moved only to In Progress and assigned exactly one same-card rework continuity.
- [ ] If no Ready, Running, or Blocked `[Codex]` Kanban tickets exist inside project scope, reported NO KANBAN CODEX WORK FOUND and stopped without edits/subagents/worktrees.
- [ ] Relevant task details/comments/dependencies inspected.
- [ ] Dependency stream map created.
- [ ] Git status recorded.
- [ ] Review/Done ownership identified.
- [ ] Parallel streams selected or exception recorded.
- [ ] One PowerShell lane orchestrator launched per safe independent `[Codex]` lane, or a `PARALLELISM EXCEPTION` recorded.
- [ ] File ownership assigned.
- [ ] Workstream packets created.
- [ ] Heavy verification sequencing decided.

Handoff checklist:
- [ ] Task scope satisfied, no adjacent tickets bundled.
- [ ] Active ticket is explicitly marked `[Codex]`.
- [ ] Actual files changed match allowed scope.
- [ ] All touched files are inside the declared project/workspace scope.
- [ ] No forbidden files changed.
- [ ] No fake evidence or fake runtime data.
- [ ] Tests/checks run or exact blocker recorded.
- [ ] Git diff checked.
- [ ] Commit created if required.
- [ ] Dirty/untracked files reported.
- [ ] Separate handoff per task.
- [ ] Next unblocked `[Codex]` streams inside project scope identified.
- [ ] Reviewer/Done ownership respected.

## Common Pitfalls

- Working serially when multiple independent `[Codex]` Ready streams exist inside project scope.
- Checking Kanban once at startup and then waiting for chat/user input instead of polling every 1 minute.
- Implementing parallel lanes in the main session instead of dispatching separate PowerShell lane orchestrators.
- Reading only Ready titles and not full dependency metadata/comments.
- Launching parallel agents that edit the same files.
- Launching or editing for an unmarked ticket that lacks `[Codex]`.
- Touching files outside the active ticket's declared project/workspace scope.
- Starting integration gates before lane terminals are approved.
- Treating user-provided streams as truth without verifying Kanban.
- Executing a pasted PRD or planned card list after Kanban shows no Ready, Running, or Blocked `[Codex]` tickets inside project scope.
- Treating old chat context as newer than Kanban.
- Bundling adjacent tickets into one broad implementation.
- Creating one mega-commit for unrelated streams.
- Running heavy builds/tests simultaneously.
- Marking Done or calling `complete`; Hermes alone owns Done.
- Fabricating test/API/model output.
- Letting one local blocker freeze unrelated safe streams.
- Leaving a Blocked `[Codex]` card parked instead of returning it to In Progress and resuming the same worker.
- Ignoring dirty worktree state.
- Forgetting to re-read Kanban after a review/block/completion.

