## Hermes Kanban CLI Command Surface

When Hermes CLI is installed and V/Hermes authorizes its use, treat it as a valid board-native Kanban read/write capability. Do not report `KANBAN TOOLING MISSING` merely because an app-native connector is absent. If the current ticket explicitly requires a different tool surface or forbids CLI use, obey the current ticket.

Before the first CLI-backed heartbeat session, or whenever command availability or mutation ownership is unclear, read `references/hermes-kanban-cli.md` completely. It contains the supported discovery, task-read, claim, comment, heartbeat, run/log, diagnostics, event-following, review, blocker, completion, read-only support, and controller command forms.

CLI operating rules:
- Run both `show` and `context` before the first edit. Rerun `show` before major transitions, review handoff, blocker handling, or successor routing.
- Use `claim` only for manually launched workers; dispatcher workers are already claimed.
- Avoid duplicate comments and heartbeats when no meaningful state or evidence changed.
- Never use `complete`. Codex posts the required review handoff; Hermes alone reviews and marks Done.
- Use `block` as a state mutation only when the active workflow permits it. Otherwise post the required `CODEX BLOCKED` comment and leave status ownership to Hermes.
- Treat controller commands as approval-gated: task creation, successor assignment/routing, dependency mutation, scheduling, promotion, archival, result backfill, specification, decomposition, notification subscription changes, and garbage collection.
- Exception: the sole autonomous status transition is the currently assigned `[Codex]` card from `Blocked` to `In Progress` (native status `running`). Reclaiming its existing continuity and launching exactly one same-card rework worker are allowed only to perform that rework. This exception does not authorize any other status change, successor routing, duplicate writers, scope expansion, destructive actions, secrets, provider spend, product-data mutation, or Done.
- A `block_loop_detected` event may cause Hermes to store the same blocked card as `triage`. For that exact recovery case only, use the minimum native promotion/claim path needed to reach `running`; this is logically the same Blocked-to-In-Progress transition, not new-card routing.
- Never use permanent archived-task deletion in an autonomous heartbeat.
- Let current V direction, ticket comments, project guardrails, and narrower safety rules override the generic allowlist.

## Dependency Stream Map

Produce this map internally before dispatch and include the relevant parts in heartbeats, comments, or handoffs when useful.

```text
DEPENDENCY STREAM MAP:
- board:
- workspace:
- project scope:
- git status:
- review policy:
  - who owns Done:
  - handoff convention:
- graph-unblocked `[Codex]` Ready tasks:
  - <id> - <title>
- active `[Codex]` Running tasks:
  - <id> - <title>
- `[Codex]` Review/For-review tasks:
  - <id> - <title>
- `[Codex]` Blocked tasks needing routing:
  - <id> - <title>

Streams:
1. <stream name>
   - root/current ticket:
   - downstream tickets:
   - owned files/directories:
   - forbidden files/directories:
   - project scope:
   - depends on:
   - blocks:
   - can run in parallel with:
   - must serialize with:
   - verification:
   - risk level:

Selected launch batch:
- <ticket/stream> -> <subagent/workstream name>

Deferred streams:
- <ticket/stream>:
  - reason deferred:
```

Stream rules:
- A stream is a chain or branch sharing a coherent subsystem, feature, file scope, or dependency lineage.
- Streams can run in parallel when all parents are done/approved, file ownership does not materially overlap, no unresolved shared API/schema/architecture decision blocks them, and required resources/tests do not conflict.
- Streams must serialize when they touch the same files/hunks, one defines a contract the other consumes, migrations/schema changes must happen first, one is an integration gate waiting for several stream terminals, or reviewer/user routing requires serialization.

## Lane Worktrees Doctrine

When executing a Hermes-planned Kanban board, inspect and classify lanes before implementation. Do not create worktrees until the lanes are proven to be real independent vertical slices.

First action:
1. Inspect Ready, Running, Review/For-review, Blocked, and relevant Todo/Backlog cards, then filter to tickets explicitly marked `[Codex]`.
   - If this finds no Ready, Running, or Blocked `[Codex]` implementation ticket, report NO KANBAN CODEX WORK FOUND with the checked statuses and stop before lane mapping, worktrees, subagents, or edits. If a Blocked card exists, reactivate and work it.
2. Read each active card body, parent/child dependencies, recent comments, and recent events/heartbeats.
3. Confirm each candidate card declares project/workspace scope and allowed files/directories. If the ticket would require touching files outside that scope, route before editing.
4. Treat Kanban dependencies as law.
5. Produce the lane map before editing files or creating worktrees.

A lane packet must include:
- lane name;
- product/module goal;
- lane-root card ID/title;
- child card IDs/titles in dependency order;
- owned files/directories;
- forbidden/overlap files/directories;
- project scope;
- upstream prerequisites;
- downstream integration gate;
- can run in parallel with;
- must not overlap with;
- narrow verification command.

Validate lane independence before creating a worktree. A lane passes only if:
- it delivers a real vertical slice, not just a technical fragment;
- it can be tested or reviewed independently;
- its file surface is mostly non-overlapping with sibling lanes;
- shared contracts are already approved or represented by an upstream gate;
- integration back into the product is explicit.

Reject fake parallelism. Do not split lanes that secretly edit the same files, same hunks, same API contract, same schema, or an unresolved architecture boundary. If candidate lanes overlap, serialize them or create/complete a prerequisite contract or integration gate first, then split.

Worktree assignment rule:
- Once a lane passes independence validation, create one branch/worktree for that lane.
- Name branches clearly after the lane, preferably `lane/<short-lane-name>`.
- Keep all edits for that lane inside its worktree.
- Do not edit sibling-lane files from this lane's worktree.
- Do not let two worktrees edit the same file unless a prior integration or contract gate explicitly allows it.

Suggested worktree pattern from the main repo root:

```powershell
git worktree add -b lane/<short-lane-name> ../worktrees/<short-lane-name> HEAD
```

Execution model:
- One lane = one worktree = one isolated workstream.
- Tickets inside a lane remain sequential according to the lane dependency chain.
- Separate lanes may run in parallel only when their owned files and contracts do not overlap.
- Heavy builds/tests should not run simultaneously on a laptop. Use narrow checks per lane and milestone/full verification at integration gates.

Required preflight report before implementation:

```text
LANE MAP
- Lane name:
- Product/module goal:
- Worktree path:
- Branch name:
- Lane-root card:
- Child cards:
- Owned files/directories:
- Forbidden/overlap files/directories:
- Project scope:
- Upstream prerequisites:
- Downstream integration gate:
- Can run in parallel with:
- Must not overlap with:
- Verification command:
- Independence verdict: PASS/FAIL
- If FAIL: serialize or name the missing contract/integration gate.
```

Implementation rules:
- Finish only the current lane card(s).
- Do not bundle future-lane work.
- Do not create fake runtime product data.
- Test doubles/fixtures are allowed only for tests and contract validation.
- Do not push.
- Do not mark Done; Hermes alone owns Done.
- When a lane card is ready, post or prepare a report beginning exactly `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:`.

Hermes lane review report:

```text
READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:
- card ID/title:
- lane name:
- worktree path:
- branch name:
- files changed:
- exact tests/checks run and output:
- commit SHA if committed:
- blockers/risks:
- downstream cards believed unblocked:
```

Integration gates:
- After sibling lanes complete, do not assume the product works.
- Rejoin lanes only through the explicit downstream integration gate.
- The integration gate must verify actual product/API behavior, not only isolated lane success.

## User-Provided Dependency Streams

If the user provides dependency streams, parse and use them as the initial stream map, then verify against Kanban before dispatch.

Accepted shapes include:

```text
STREAM A: Backend/API
- t_aaa: Add schema
- t_bbb: Add route
- t_ccc: Add route tests

STREAM B: Frontend/UI
- t_ddd: Add types
- t_eee: Add component
- t_fff: Add UI tests

GATES:
- t_ggg: integration test waits for STREAM A + STREAM B
```

```text
Parallel lanes:
1. t_123 -> t_456 -> t_789
2. t_abc -> t_def
3. t_xyz
Gate: t_gate waits for lane terminals.
```

When streams are provided:
1. Normalize them into the stream map.
   - If the stream tasks do not exist on Kanban, or the cards were planned but not created/promoted, report the mismatch and stop. Do not execute against uncreated cards.

