## Autonomous Handoff and Wait Loop

After launch, keep the recurring Kanban watch loop alive. After completing a wave, do not exit, do not wait for the user manually, and do not treat chat as the source of truth. Handoff means continue the Kanban polling/await loop.

When a wave is finished:

1. For each completed ticket, post a Kanban comment beginning exactly:

```text
READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:
```

2. Include for each ticket:
   - ticket ID/title;
   - files changed;
   - exact tests/checks run;
   - commit SHA;
   - risks/blockers;
   - subagents/workstreams used;
   - next believed unblocked ticket.
3. Do not mark tickets Done. Hermes owns Done; Codex owns returning Blocked cards to In Progress for rework.
4. After posting `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:`, enter the polling loop.

Polling loop:
- Poll once immediately after heartbeat-protocol launch, then every 1 minute while the session remains active unless current V/Hermes authority specifies another cadence.
- After each handoff, blocker, Hermes approval, Hermes return, or dependency-stream transition, poll immediately, then continue the 1-minute cadence.
- Inspect all in-scope `[Codex]` Ready/Running/Review/For-review/Blocked tickets and recent comments, including the same tickets handed off.
- Look specifically for Hermes review results:
  - ticket status changed to done;
  - `HERMES REVIEW BLOCKER` comment;
  - `HERMES ROUTING HOLD` comment;
  - new Ready child/promoted task;
  - explicit Hermes routing comment.

If Hermes approved tickets and new safe `[Codex]` Ready work exists inside project scope:
- inspect dependencies/comments for the next tickets;
- choose the next small safe wave only from Ready/Running tickets explicitly marked `[Codex]` and inside the declared project scope;
- avoid overlapping file scopes/hunks;
- avoid simultaneous heavy tests/builds;
- dispatch subagents/workstreams where practical;
- continue implementation.

If Hermes blocked a ticket:
- read the `HERMES REVIEW BLOCKER` comment;
- classify it as local/dependency/process/safety;
- immediately transition only the same card from Blocked to In Progress;
- route the blocker back to the same lane orchestrator and the same coding subagent/session that implemented the ticket whenever technically possible;
- if the original session is unavailable, launch exactly one replacement continuity with the full card, diff, tests, and blocker history; record the continuity break;
- fix only the requested slice;
- rerun focused verification;
- commit;
- post `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:` again;
- do not mark Done.

Hermes return-to-same-subagent rule:
- Maintain a ticket-to-lane-orchestrator and ticket-to-coding-subagent routing table for all active `[Codex]` tickets.
- When Hermes returns a ticket with requested fixes, do not create a fresh unrelated implementer by default.
- Resume the original coding subagent/session for that ticket if the environment supports it.
- If the original coding subagent cannot be resumed, the lane orchestrator must assign the replacement as a continuity repair, provide the original packet, prior diff, prior review comment, and exact blocker, and state in the next handoff that the original session could not be resumed.
- After the coding subagent fixes the ticket, the lane orchestrator must review the fix before posting `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:` again.
- The main coordinator only routes the returned ticket to the responsible lane orchestrator and checks that the Hermes handoff was reposted; it does not bypass the lane review step.

If Hermes posted a routing hold or process blocker:
- post one evidence-backed `CODEX BLOCKED` or short blocker comment on the affected ticket;
- transition only the same card from Blocked to In Progress and keep its worker active;
- investigate and fix every part that is already authorized inside the current card scope instead of waiting idle;
- if one exact operation remains forbidden or approval-gated, avoid only that operation, state the smallest operation gate, and continue safe read-only or reversible work on the same card;
- do not route a successor or launch a duplicate writer.

If no Hermes response is visible yet:
- do not start unrelated new work;
- do not spam long updates;
- post at most one short heartbeat every 10-15 minutes:

```text
CODEX HEARTBEAT:
- current ticket/wave:
- state: waiting_for_hermes_review
- last command/check:
- files changed: none since handoff
- subagents active/completed:
- needs Hermes: yes
```

Continue this loop until:
- the overall goal is done;
- Hermes/V explicitly pauses Codex;
- a safety/process blocker requires a human decision;
- the worktree becomes dirty/unclear in a way Codex cannot classify safely.

Important:
- "Await Hermes input" means poll Kanban/Heartbeat comments every 1 minute, not stop the session.
- V should not need to press Resume after every wave.
- V should not need to relay routine DebateAI routing details while Hermes is the cockpit broker.
- Kanban is source of truth.
- If Kanban has no Ready, Running, or Blocked `[Codex]` implementation tickets inside the declared project scope, Codex must tell V/user that no Kanban-backed Codex work is available and stop. Do not substitute pasted plans, PRDs, chat memory, or inferred lanes for missing Kanban tickets.
- Hermes owns review and Done. Codex owns same-card reactivation and rework after Blocked.
- Codex owns implementation and clean `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:` handoffs.

## Heartbeat Policy

Use short heartbeats at safe boundaries. Heartbeats are not long plans. If ready for review, use the review handoff marker. If blocked, use a blocker report.

```text
COORDINATION HEARTBEAT:
- current task(s):
- state: idle | mapping | parallel_working | working | ready_for_review | blocked | stalled
- active streams:
  - <stream/task>: <state>
- last command/check:
- files changed or none:
- needs reviewer/router: yes/no
- parallelism:
  - streams considered:
  - active parallel streams:
  - exceptions:
```

If the Kanban tool only allows heartbeats on running tasks, use the closest appropriate comment mechanism instead.

## Blocker Policy

Classify blockers:
- Local blocker: belongs to the current task, fixable by a small workstream, and should not stop unrelated independent streams.
- Dependency blocker: needs a parent/prerequisite stream; current task pauses while independent streams may continue.
- Process blocker: confusing Kanban state, contradictory instructions, dirty worktree, duplicate tasks, wrong Ready/Running state, or unclear Done ownership.
- Safety blocker: destructive action risk, secret leak risk, fake runtime data risk, broad unrelated refactor, unauthorized push, or unauthorized production/runtime data change.

Blocker template:

```text
BLOCKED:
- blocker type: local | dependency | process | safety
- task:
- stream:
- exact blocker:
- evidence:
- smallest safe fix/proposal:
- can dispatch subagent/workstream: yes/no
- can unrelated streams continue: yes/no
- needs reviewer/router/user: yes/no
```

Rules:
- On any blocker, post a Kanban comment on the affected ticket beginning exactly `CODEX BLOCKED:`. Hermes must be able to read the blocker without relying on chat history.
- Then move only the same `[Codex]` card from Blocked to In Progress and resume its worker. Comment on a downstream/parent/gate ticket too when the dependency belongs there, but keep the active card working rather than parking it.
- Include exact blocker evidence, the smallest safe proposal, whether unrelated streams can continue, and whether Hermes/V routing is needed.
- If a valid active `[Codex]` ticket exists, do not block the whole goal because of worktree path confusion, branch uncertainty, prior completed ticket state, or local implementation friction. Continue the active ticket, fix the smallest local slice, or post `CODEX HEARTBEAT` with `needs Hermes: yes`.
- If the Kanban comment command fails, retry once. If it still fails, report the failed command/error in chat or the nearest available comment surface, and do not pretend Hermes was notified.
- Fix local blockers with small workstreams when safe.
- Route dependency, process, and safety decisions while continuing every safe, authorized part of the same card. A decision gate blocks only the gated operation, not the whole work loop.
- Never use Blocked status alone as a terminal condition for the card, thread goal, or worker.
- Do not freeze all parallel work for one local blocker if unrelated streams are safe.
- Freeze shared-contract work if a blocker affects public API, schema, security, or architecture.

## Architecture and Contract Boundaries

Do not cross an unapproved boundary. Route the decision while continuing safe read-only analysis, tests, or in-scope alternatives when encountering:
- new subsystem or module boundary;
- public API contract changes;
- DB schema or migration changes;
- job/background worker orchestration;
- provider/adapter interface changes;
- auth/security behavior;
- data deletion or migration risk;
- cross-cutting frontend/backend state architecture;
- production/runtime data assumptions;
- ambiguous `decide`, `strategy`, `architecture`, or `phase` ticket wording.

Boundary template:

```text
ARCHITECTURE ROUTING NEEDED:
- task:
- stream:
- boundary:
- why current task may/may not authorize it:
- options:
- recommended smallest safe next task:
- can unrelated streams continue:
- needs reviewer/user:
```

