## Verification Policy

- Run the narrowest relevant checks per task.
- Do not fabricate command output.
- If verification cannot run, report the exact blocker.
- Avoid simultaneous heavy tests/builds.
- Use lane-local tests for implementation streams.
- Use broader checks only at integration/milestone gates.
- Include exact command and real output in handoff.

Verification report:

```text
VERIFICATION:
- command:
- exit code:
- output summary:
- full relevant output:
- artifacts/generated files:
```

## Multi-Stream Completion Flow

When parallel workstreams finish:
1. Coordinator collects reports.
2. Coordinator checks git status.
3. Coordinator integrates one stream at a time.
4. Coordinator runs narrow verification for each stream.
5. Coordinator commits per task if required.
6. Coordinator posts/prepares separate review handoffs per task.
7. Coordinator re-reads Kanban after each approval, block, or handoff.
8. Coordinator launches newly unblocked independent `[Codex]` streams inside declared project scope if safe.
9. Coordinator does not start integration gates until all required stream terminals are approved/done.

If several streams finish at once, prefer separate commits, separate handoffs, separate evidence, and explicit next-task mapping.

## Checklists

Start checklist:
- [ ] Board/workspace identified.
- [ ] Declared project/workspace scope identified.
- [ ] Recurring Kanban watch loop started: first poll immediately, then every 1 minute while active unless current V/Hermes authority specifies another cadence.
- [ ] Kanban Ready/Running/Review/Blocked inspected.
- [ ] Tickets filtered to explicit `[Codex]` ownership before implementation selection.
- [ ] Every Blocked `[Codex]` card was moved only to In Progress and assigned exactly one same-card rework continuity.
- [ ] If no Ready, Running, or Blocked `[Codex]` Kanban tickets exist inside project scope, reported NO KANBAN CODEX WORK FOUND and stopped without edits/subagents/worktrees.
- [ ] Relevant task details/comments/dependencies inspected.
- [ ] Dependency stream map created.
- [ ] Git status recorded.
- [ ] Review/Done ownership identified.
- [ ] Parallel streams selected or exception recorded.
- [ ] One PowerShell lane orchestrator launched per safe independent `[Codex]` lane, or a `PARALLELISM EXCEPTION` recorded.
- [ ] File ownership assigned.
- [ ] Workstream packets created.
- [ ] Heavy verification sequencing decided.

Handoff checklist:
- [ ] Task scope satisfied, no adjacent tickets bundled.
- [ ] Active ticket is explicitly marked `[Codex]`.
- [ ] Actual files changed match allowed scope.
- [ ] All touched files are inside the declared project/workspace scope.
- [ ] No forbidden files changed.
- [ ] No fake evidence or fake runtime data.
- [ ] Tests/checks run or exact blocker recorded.
- [ ] Git diff checked.
- [ ] Commit created if required.
- [ ] Dirty/untracked files reported.
- [ ] Separate handoff per task.
- [ ] Next unblocked `[Codex]` streams inside project scope identified.
- [ ] Reviewer/Done ownership respected.

## Common Pitfalls

- Working serially when multiple independent `[Codex]` Ready streams exist inside project scope.
- Checking Kanban once at startup and then waiting for chat/user input instead of polling every 1 minute.
- Implementing parallel lanes in the main session instead of dispatching separate PowerShell lane orchestrators.
- Reading only Ready titles and not full dependency metadata/comments.
- Launching parallel agents that edit the same files.
- Launching or editing for an unmarked ticket that lacks `[Codex]`.
- Touching files outside the active ticket's declared project/workspace scope.
- Starting integration gates before lane terminals are approved.
- Treating user-provided streams as truth without verifying Kanban.
- Executing a pasted PRD or planned card list after Kanban shows no Ready, Running, or Blocked `[Codex]` tickets inside project scope.
- Treating old chat context as newer than Kanban.
- Bundling adjacent tickets into one broad implementation.
- Creating one mega-commit for unrelated streams.
- Running heavy builds/tests simultaneously.
- Marking Done or calling `complete`; Hermes alone owns Done.
- Fabricating test/API/model output.
- Letting one local blocker freeze unrelated safe streams.
- Leaving a Blocked `[Codex]` card parked instead of returning it to In Progress and resuming the same worker.
- Ignoring dirty worktree state.
- Forgetting to re-read Kanban after a review/block/completion.

